## 1.2 Hardware Architecture

This section presents the hardware architecture of the automatic ice cream dispensing module and its connection to the Edge Computer in the kiosk system. The hardware architecture is divided into five main units: Control Unit, Communication Unit, Power Supply Unit, Actuator Unit, and Limit Sensor Unit.

**[Chèn Hình 1.2.1 tại đây]**

**Figure 1.2.1 – Hardware Interconnection Diagram of the Automatic Ice Cream Dispensing Module**

Figure 1.2.1 illustrates the overall interconnection between the hardware components. The Edge Computer communicates with the system through the USB-to-RS485 Converter, which converts data from the Edge Computer’s USB interface into RS485 signals.
The RS485 signal is transmitted through the differential A/B communication line to the TTL-to-RS485 Converter Module (MAX485). The module provides bidirectional conversion between differential RS485 signals and serial logic-level signals on the TTL side, connecting to the STM32 through USART1 in UART mode. The Edge Computer is connected to the RS485 bus through the USB-to-RS485 Converter.
STM32 pin PA4/A4 connects to both DE and /RE (active-low receiver enable) on the MAX485 to control the direction of half-duplex RS485 communication. PA4 HIGH selects transmit mode, while PA4 LOW selects receive mode.
The STM32 provides PWM signals through pins PA8 and PB1. These PWM signals pass through two PC817 Optocouplers before reaching the BTS7960 Motor Driver Module. The BTS7960 uses these signals to control the operation of the JGB37-520 Geared DC Motor.
The JGB37-520 motor is connected to the Lead Screw Mechanism. This mechanism converts the motor’s rotational motion into linear motion, thereby opening and closing the ice cream dispensing mechanism.
Two KW12-071 mechanical limit switches provide travel-limit feedback to the STM32: the Upper Limit Switch connects to PB0/B0, and the Lower Limit Switch connects to PB10/B10. In the diagram, each GPIO connects through a series resistor R to the corresponding COM terminal, while NO connects to the STM32-side GND. Both inputs use the STM32 internal pull-up resistors. Pressing a switch closes COM–NO to pull the input LOW (active-low). The switches identify the upper and lower travel endpoints of the mechanism.
The STM32 uses these limit signals to control the BTS7960 by direction. When the Upper Limit Switch is active, upward motion is stopped and further upward motion is inhibited; when the Lower Limit Switch is active, downward motion is stopped and further downward motion is inhibited. Motion in the opposite direction remains available to move the mechanism away from the limit, provided that the limit switch for that direction is not active. When both switches are active, motion in both directions is inhibited.
Regarding the power supply, the 24V Switching Power Supply converts 220V AC into 24V DC to supply the BTS7960 motor power input and the motor. The LM2596 Step-Down Voltage Regulator converts the 24V supply into a separate 5V rail for the BTS7960 logic side and the output side of the PC817 optocouplers. The 5V Power Adapter supplies both the STM32 and the TTL-to-RS485 Converter Module (MAX485). In the hardware configuration used here, the STM32 board and MAX485 module receive a 5V supply, while the serial connection between the boards uses 3.3V logic levels as confirmed for the actual module. The PC817 optocouplers electrically isolate the STM32/control-side ground from the motor-driver-side control signals.

