### 1.2.6 Communication Standardization and Extensibility

The custom control circuit presented in this document was developed to convert a manually operated ice cream machine into an automatic mechanism. This design serves as a reference implementation for the specific ice cream machine and is not a mandatory hardware configuration for all devices integrated into the kiosk system.
The system uses RS485 as the common physical interface for peripherals directly controlled by the Edge Computer. Each device requires a compatible RS485 interface and may use its own controller, power supply, sensors, and actuators. Protocol and software integration requirements are covered in the corresponding documentation.
Using RS485 as the standard communication interface provides a consistent connection method between the Edge Computer and peripheral devices. The Edge Computer communicates with the RS485 bus through the USB-to-RS485 Converter, while controllers such as the STM32 connect through UART on the logic side of the TTL-to-RS485 Converter Module (MAX485). This design reduces differences between device types, simplifies the integration process, and supports system scalability.
Therefore, the diagrams in this section describe a specific implementation for the ice cream machine while also providing a reference for integrating other peripheral devices into the kiosk system.
